ed from Google Drive using !gdown.\n\nLoad: The data was read into a Pandas DataFrame, showing a shape of 200 rows and 5 columns.\n\nInspect: The first 5 rows display customer attributes: Gender, Age, Annual Income, and Spending Score.\n\nCleanliness: A check for null values confirms the dataset is completely clean with zero missing entries.\nNo missing values detected across any column — the dataset is clean and ready for preprocessing.","metadata":{"id":"null_note"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4. Preprocessing","metadata":{"id":"prep_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\ndf.drop(columns=[\"CustomerID\"], inplace=True)\n\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=[\"Gender\"], drop_first=True)\n\ndf.head()","metadata":{"id":"preprocess_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.061406Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.061664Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.078098Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.061636Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.077544Z"}},"outputs":[{"execution_count":8,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"   Age  Annual Income (k$)  Spending Score (1-100)  Gender_Male\n0   19                  15                      39         True\n1   21                  15                      81         True\n2   20                  16                       6        False\n3   23                  16                      77        False\n4   31                  17                      40        False","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>Age</th>\n      <th>Annual Income (k$)</th>\n      <th>Spending Score (1-100)</th>\n      <th>Gender_Male</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>19</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>39</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>21</td>\n      <td>15</td>\n      <td>81</td>\n      <td>True</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>20</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>6</td>\n      <td>False</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>23</td>\n      <td>16</td>\n      <td>77</td>\n      <td>False</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>31</td>\n      <td>17</td>\n      <td>40</td>\n      <td>False</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\nAcquisition of 'Mall_Customers.csv' via !gdown, validation of zero null values, and removal of irrelevant CustomerID.\nCategorical Gender is transformed into numerical Gender_Male using one-hot encoding, preparing the data for clustering algorithms.\nThese essential preprocessing steps within Kaggle ensure a clean and optimized numerical dataset for model training.","metadata":{"id":"encode_note"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Feature Scaling","metadata":{"id":"scale_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"scaler = StandardScaler()\nX = scaler.fit_transform(df)  \nprint(f\"Scaled matrix shape: {X.shape}\")\nprint(f\"Feature means (should be ~0): {X.mean(axis=0).round(3)}\")\nprint(f\"Feature stds  (should be ~1): {X.std(axis=0).round(3)}\")","metadata":{"id":"scale_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.078890Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.079130Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.091338Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.079112Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.090767Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Scaled matrix shape: (200, 4)\nFeature means (should be ~0): [-0. -0. -0.  0.]\nFeature stds  (should be ~1): [1. 1. 1. 1.]\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Z-score standardization centers each feature at 0 with a standard deviation of 1, ensuring balanced contributions to the model.\nImplementing StandardScaler in Kaggle prevents features with large numeric ranges, like Annual Income, from dominating Euclidean distance.\nThis transformation ensures Age, Annual Income, and Spending Score carry equal mathematical weight during the clustering process.\nThe final 200x4 scaled matrix provides a uniform foundation, making the data ready for immediate and accurate algorithm application.","metadata":{"id":"scale_note"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Choosing Optimal K for K-Means (Elbow Method)","metadata":{"id":"elbow_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"K_RANGE = range(1, 30)\n\n\nwcss_scores = [\n    KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42, n_init=10).fit(X).inertia_\n    for k in K_RANGE\n]\n\n# ── Elbow plot ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 4))\nax.plot(K_RANGE, wcss_scores, color=\"steelblue\", linewidth=2,\n        marker=\"s\", markersize=5, markerfacecolor=\"white\", markeredgewidth=1.5)\nax.axvline(x=11, color=\"tomato\", linestyle=\"--\", linewidth=1.5, label=\"Optimal K = 11\")\nax.set_title(\"Elbow Method – Within-Cluster Sum of Squares\", fontsize=13, pad=10)\nax.set_xlabel(\"Number of Clusters (K)\", fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel(\"WCSS (Inertia)\", fontsize=11)\nax.set_xticks(K_RANGE)\nax.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"id":"elbow_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.092162Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.092540Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.410479Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:49.092498Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.409748Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 990x440 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n\n- We applied the **Elbow Method** to determine the optimal number of clusters.\n- The WCSS decreases sharply at the beginning and then starts to level off.\n- The curve forms an elbow around **K = 11**.\n- Therefore, **11 clusters** is selected as the optimal number of clusters.\n- After K = 11, the reduction in WCSS becomes minimal.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"OPTIMAL_K = 11   ","metadata":{"id":"optimal_k_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.411312Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.411628Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.414919Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.411604Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.414253Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":11},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The dashed red line marks K = 11, the point at which additional clusters yield diminishing reductions in WCSS, making it the best trade-off between model complexity and explanatory power.","metadata":{"id":"elbow_note"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. Applying K-Means","metadata":{"id":"kmeans_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"kmeans_model  = KMeans(n_clusters=OPTIMAL_K, random_state=42, n_init=10)\nkmeans_labels = kmeans_model.fit_predict(X)\nprint(f\"KMeans cluster distribution:\\n{pd.Series(kmeans_labels).value_counts().sort_index()}\")","metadata":{"id":"kmeans_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.416007Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.416202Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.458512Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.416184Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.457967Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"KMeans cluster distribution:\n0     20\n1     22\n2     13\n3     21\n4     26\n5     20\n6     25\n7     18\n8     13\n9     14\n10     8\nName: count, dtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":12},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## K-Means Cluster Distribution\n\nThe K-Means algorithm was applied using the optimal value **K = 11**.  \nThe output shows the number of data points assigned to each cluster.  \nClusters are reasonably distributed, with sizes ranging from **8 to 26** samples.  \nThis indicates a balanced segmentation without extreme imbalance.  \nThe clustering structure appears stable and well-partitioned.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 8. Applying DBSCAN","metadata":{"id":"dbscan_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\nsearch_results = []\n\nfor eps_int in range(1, 201):\n    eps = eps_int / 10\n    for min_pts in range(2, 20):\n        lbl = DBSCAN(eps=eps, min_samples=min_pts).fit_predict(X)\n        n_clusters = len(set(lbl) - {-1})   # exclude noise label\n        n_noise    = (lbl == -1).sum()\n        sil        = silhouette_score(X, lbl) if n_clusters > 1 else -1\n\n        search_results.append({\n            \"eps\": eps, \"min_samples\": min_pts,\n            \"n_clusters\": n_clusters, \"n_noise\": n_noise,\n            \"silhouette\": sil\n        })\n\nsearch_df = (\n    pd.DataFrame(search_results)\n      .sort_values([\"silhouette\", \"n_noise\", \"min_samples\", \"eps\"],\n                   ascending=[False, False, True, True])\n)\nprint(search_df.head(10).to_string(index=False))","metadata":{"id":"dbscan_search_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.460242Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.460490Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.556746Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:50.460471Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.555972Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":" eps  min_samples  n_clusters  n_noise  silhouette\n 1.1            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.2            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.3            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.4            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.5            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.6            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.7            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.8            2           2        0    0.277604\n 1.9            2           2        0    0.277604\n 2.0            2           2        0    0.277604\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":13},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The optimal parameter combination shows **eps values between 1.1 and 2.0** with **min_samples = 2**.  \nThis configuration produces **2 clusters** with **0 noise points**.  \nThe achieved **silhouette score is 0.2776**, indicating moderate cluster separation.  \nThe consistency across multiple eps values suggests stable density-based clustering.  \nNo data points were classified as noise under this configuration.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\ndbscan_model  = DBSCAN(eps=1.1, min_samples=2)\ndbscan_labels = dbscan_model.fit_predict(X)\nprint(f\"DBSCAN clusters found : {len(set(dbscan_labels) - {-1})}\")\nprint(f\"Noise points           : {(dbscan_labels == -1).sum()}\")","metadata":{"id":"dbscan_fit_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.557605Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.557862Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.564471Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.557841Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.563770Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"DBSCAN clusters found : 2\nNoise points           : 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":14},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Final DBSCAN Model\n\nDBSCAN was applied with **eps = 1.1** and **min_samples = 2**.  \nThe model identified **2 clusters** in the dataset.  \nThe number of detected **noise points is 0**, meaning all samples were assigned to clusters.  \nThis confirms a clean density-based separation under the selected parameters.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 9. Applying GMM","metadata":{"id":"gmm_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"\nbic_scores = []\ncomponent_range = range(2, 25)\n\nfor n in component_range:\n    gmm_tmp = GaussianMixture(n_components=n, random_state=42, n_init=3)\n    gmm_tmp.fit(X)\n    bic_scores.append(gmm_tmp.bic(X))\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 4))\nax.plot(component_range, bic_scores, color=\"mediumseagreen\", linewidth=2,\n        marker=\"o\", markersize=5, markerfacecolor=\"white\", markeredgewidth=1.5)\nax.set_title(\"GMM – BIC Score vs Number of Components\", fontsize=13, pad=10)\nax.set_xlabel(\"Number of Components\", fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel(\"BIC\", fontsize=11)\nax.set_xticks(component_range)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n\nOPTIMAL_GMM_K = component_range[int(np.argmin(bic_scores))]\nprint(f\"Best n_components by BIC: {OPTIMAL_GMM_K}\")","metadata":{"id":"gmm_bic_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.565432Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.565915Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.074837Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:45:56.565894Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.074184Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 880x440 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"Best n_components by BIC: 2\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":15},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## GMM Model Selection using BIC\n\nGaussian Mixture Models were evaluated for different numbers of components.  \nThe **Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC)** was computed for each model.  \nThe optimal number of components corresponds to the **minimum BIC value**.  \nFrom the results, the best choice is **n_components = 2**.  \nThis indicates that a 2-component Gaussian mixture provides the best balance between model fit and complexity.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"gmm_model  = GaussianMixture(n_components=OPTIMAL_K, random_state=42)\ngmm_labels = gmm_model.fit_predict(X)","metadata":{"id":"gmm_fit_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.075682Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.075962Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.124402Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.075916Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.123866Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":16},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 10. Cluster Evaluation","metadata":{"id":"eval_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def compute_metrics(data, labels):\n    \"\"\"Return silhouette score and Davies–Bouldin index for a label assignment.\"\"\"\n    if len(set(labels)) < 2:\n        return None, None\n    return silhouette_score(data, labels), davies_bouldin_score(data, labels)\n\nmodel_names  = [\"KMeans\", \"DBSCAN\", \"GMM\"]\nlabel_arrays = [kmeans_labels, dbscan_labels, gmm_labels]\n\neval_rows = []\nfor name, lbl in zip(model_names, label_arrays):\n    sil, dbi = compute_metrics(X, lbl)\n    eval_rows.append({\"Model\": name,\n                      \"Silhouette ↑\": round(sil, 4) if sil else \"N/A\",\n                      \"Davies-Bouldin ↓\": round(dbi, 4) if dbi else \"N/A\"})\n\neval_df = pd.DataFrame(eval_rows).set_index(\"Model\")\nprint(eval_df.to_string())","metadata":{"id":"eval_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.125017Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.125235Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.153387Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.125212Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.152798Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"        Silhouette ↑  Davies-Bouldin ↓\nModel                                 \nKMeans        0.4275            0.8416\nDBSCAN        0.2776            1.6117\nGMM           0.4070            0.8855\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":17},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Cluster Evaluation Summary\n\n**KMeans** achieves the highest **Silhouette score** and the lowest **Davies–Bouldin Index**, indicating the most compact and well-separated clusters.  \n\n**GMM** produces comparable performance with slightly lower separation quality.  \n\n**DBSCAN** underperforms in this case, as forming only **two large clusters** reduces granularity and limits cluster differentiation.","metadata":{"id":"eval_note"}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 11. Visualization – PCA Projection","metadata":{"id":"pca_header"}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pca       = PCA(n_components=2, random_state=42)\npca_coords = pca.fit_transform(X)\nprint(f\"Explained variance ratio: {pca.explained_variance_ratio_.round(3)} \"\n      f\"(total: {pca.explained_variance_ratio_.sum():.1%})\")\n\npca_df = pd.DataFrame(pca_coords, columns=[\"PC1\", \"PC2\"])\npca_df[[\"KMeans\", \"DBSCAN\", \"GMM\"]] = pd.DataFrame(\n    {\"KMeans\": kmeans_labels, \"DBSCAN\": dbscan_labels, \"GMM\": gmm_labels}\n)","metadata":{"id":"pca_transform_cell","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.154054Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.154361Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.171920Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.154336Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-02-23T06:46:00.170488Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Explained variance ratio: [0.337 0.262] (total: 59.9%)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":18},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## PCA Projection Summary\n\nPrincipal Component Analysis (PCA) was applied to reduce the data to **two dimensions**.  \nThe first two principal components (**PC1** and **PC2**) explain **59.9%** of the total variance.  \nThis projection preserves most of the data structure while enabling clear 2D visualization.  \nCluster labels from **KMeans**, **DBSCAN**, and **GMM** are mapped onto the PCA space.  \nThe visualization helps compare cluster separation and overlap across different algorithms.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"PALETTE = \"tab10\"\nDOT_SIZE = 60\